==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|bewteful}}, {{m|enm|beautefull||attractive to the eye, beautiful}}, equivalent to {{suf|en|beauty|ful|pos=adjective}}. In this sense, largely displaced {{noncog|ang|fæġer}} (whence {{m|en|fair}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{enPR|byo͞oʹtĭ-fəl}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈbjuːtɪ.fəl/|/ˈbjuːtɪ.fʊl/}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-beautiful.ogg|Audio (UK)}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbju.tɪ.fəl/|[bju.ɾə.fəl]}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-beautiful.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{hyphenation|en|beau|ti|ful}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Attractive and possessing [[beauty]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=5|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=It's rather like a '''beautiful''' Inverness cloak one has inherited. Much too good to hide away, so one wears it instead of an overcoat and pretends it's an amusing new fashion.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=2016|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170930001420/https://learningenglish.voanews.com/a/lets-learn-english-lesson-5-where-are-you/3168971.html|title=VOA Learning English (public domain)|text=It is a '''beautiful''' kitchen! &mdash; It ''is'' '''beautiful'''.}}
#*:: {{audio|en|It is a beautiful kitchen! It is beautiful.ogg|Audio (US)}}
#: {{ux|en|Anyone who has ever met her thought she was absolutely '''beautiful'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|There's a '''beautiful''' lake by the town.}}
# Good, admirable.
#: {{ux|en|He was a '''beautiful''' person; he would drop everything to help you.}}
#: {{ux|en|You've done a '''beautiful''' thing today.}}
# {{lb|en|of the weather}} Pleasant; [[clear]].
#: {{ux|en|It's '''beautiful''' outside, let's go for a walk.}}
# [[well|Well]] [[executed]].
#: {{ux|en|The skater performed a '''beautiful''' [[axel]].}}

====Usage notes====
* When used to refer to human appearance, the word is more commonly used for women, with {{m|en|handsome}} being more common for men, though neither is incorrect. For a man, ''beautiful'' could connote a more delicate or androgynous appearance.
* The comparatives '''[[beautifuler]]''' and '''[[beautifuller]]''', and the superlatives '''[[beautifulest]]''' and '''[[beautifullest]]''' have also occasionally been used, but are nonstandard.

